---
title: 'Cross Lines'
description: 'Axes Cross Lines are lines or shaded areas in a $framework chart that provide additional info or thresholds to aid data analysis. Supports Cartesian and Polar Axes.'
---

Cross Lines are lines or shaded areas in a chart that denote additional information or thresholds, making them useful for data analysis.

## Adding Cross Lines

To add a Cross Line at a specific data value on an axis, use the `crossLines` property in the `axis` options.

A Cross Line can either be a `line` or a `range`, and multiple Cross Lines can be added to a single axis.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Adding Cross Lines" name="axis-cross-lines-adding" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    axes: {
        y: {
            type: 'number',
            crossLines: [
                {
                    type: 'line',
                    value: 11,
                },
            ],
        },
        x: {
            type: 'category',
            crossLines: [
                {
                    type: 'range',
                    range: ['Jun', 'Sep'],
                },
            ],
        },
    },
}
```

In this configuration:

- A `line` is displayed on the vertical axis at value 11.
- A `range` is displayed on the horizontal axis between June and September.
- The `value` or `range` values should be the same types as those displayed in the axis.

## Customising Cross Lines

{% chartExampleRunner title="Customising Cross Lines" name="axis-cross-lines-customising" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    crossLines: [
        {
            type: 'range',
            range: [new Date(2019, 4, 1), new Date(2019, 6, 1)],
            strokeWidth: 0,
            fill: '#7290C4',
            fillOpacity: 0.4,
            label: {
                text: 'Price Peak',
                placement: 'top',
                fontSize: 14,
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this configuration:

- Properties such as `stroke`, `strokeWidth`, and `fill` are used to customise the Cross Line.
- A label is added, customised and placed in relation to the Cross Line. See [Label Placement](#label-placement) for more details.

## Label Placement

Use `label.placement` to choose where a label sits relative to its Cross Line. The default is `top`.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Label Placement" name="label-placement" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    axes: {
        x: {
            type: 'time',
            crossLines: [
                {
                    type: 'line',
                    value: new Date(2025, 9, 1),
                    label: { text: 'Launch', placement: 'left-top' },
                },
            ],
        },
        y: {
            type: 'number',
            crossLines: [
                {
                    type: 'range',
                    range: [180, 210],
                    label: { text: 'Baseline', placement: 'inside-top-left' },
                },
            ],
        },
    },
}
```

In this example:

- Use the checkboxes to show a `line` and a `range` on each axis, and the dropdowns to change their placements. Each dropdown lists the placements that apply to that Cross Line.
- The first part of a placement names the edge the label sits against, and the second positions it along that edge. For example, `top-left` sits above the Cross Line aligned to its left, while `left-top` sits beside its left edge aligned to its top.
- Some placements apply to only one axis, such as `top-left` for a y-axis line and `left-top` for an x-axis line.
- `start` and `end` resolve to `left` and `right`, and are mirrored when [RTL Layout](./rtl/) is enabled.
- Providing `placement` as an ordered array lets the label [fall back](./series-labels/#placement) to the next placement when it collides or doesn't fit.

### Fitting Long Labels

There are a number of ways to handle long labels which do not fit within the series area or exceed a provided `maxWidth` or `maxHeight`.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Fitting Long Labels" name="label-fitting" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    crossLines: [
        {
            type: 'range',
            range: [new Date(2025, 5, 1), new Date(2025, 7, 1)],
            label: {
                text: 'Summer Promotion: Reduced Pricing in All Stores',
                placement: 'inside-top',
                maxWidth: 120,
                maxHeight: 60,
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this example:

- Use the controls to see how the labels wrap, shrink and truncate.
- A label that exceeds `maxWidth` or `maxHeight` or does not fit within the series area, wraps according to `wrapping`, shrinks towards `minimumFontSize`, then is truncated if `truncate` is enabled. See [Series Label Collision Avoidance](./series-labels/#collision-avoidance) for details.

## Polar Axes Cross Lines

Cross Lines are also available for Polar Axes. They share the line and range options of Cartesian Cross Lines, but the label options differ by axis: radius axis labels are positioned with `positionAngle`, and angle axis labels support the common label options only.

{% note %}
Polar Cross Lines are not included in `CrossLinesModule`. Register `PolarCrossLinesModule` separately when using [module imports](./module-registry/).
{% /note %}

{% chartExampleRunner title="Polar Axes Cross Lines" name="polar-axes-crosslines-angle" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    axes: {
        angle: {
            type: 'angle-category',
            shape: 'circle',
            crossLines: [
                {
                    type: 'range',
                    range: ['Technical Skills', 'Communication'],
                    label: {
                        text: 'Valuable Skills',
                    },
                },
            ],
        },
        radius: {
            type: 'radius-number',
            shape: 'circle',
            crossLines: [
                {
                    type: 'line',
                    stroke: 'red',
                    value: 6,
                    label: {
                        text: 'Minimal\nRequirement',
                        positionAngle: 180,
                    },
                },
            ],
        },
    },
}
```

## Theming Cross Lines

[Theme overrides](./themes/#overrides) style every Cross Line on an axis type. Add a `line` or `range` object to style only the Cross Lines of that type.

```js format="snippet"
{
    theme: {
        overrides: {
            common: {
                axes: {
                    number: {
                        crossLines: {
                            strokeWidth: 2,
                            line: { stroke: '#d62728', lineDash: [4, 4] },
                            range: { fill: '#7290C4', fillOpacity: 0.2 },
                        },
                    },
                },
            },
        },
    },
}
```

In this configuration:

- `strokeWidth` applies to every Cross Line.
- The `line` and `range` options take precedence over the options for every Cross Line, and options set on a Cross Line take precedence over both.

